{
  "case_id": "2011-11-94",
  "document_hash": "2cf2ed3e46f7d98087f0a5a4d5573cd99621d1ab7d7e88df5134d20f8020d4d9",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2011-11-94",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cf2ed3e46f7d98087f0a5a4d5573cd99621d1ab7d7e88df5134d20f8020d4d9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cf2ed3e46f7-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:05 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2011-11-94",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cf2ed3e46f7d98087f0a5a4d5573cd99621d1ab7d7e88df5134d20f8020d4d9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cf2ed3e46f7-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Was that jury trial constitutionally required?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2011-11-94",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cf2ed3e46f7d98087f0a5a4d5573cd99621d1ab7d7e88df5134d20f8020d4d9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cf2ed3e46f7-0000-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Congress is quite explicit that the maximum fine for a single day's violation is $50,000."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2011-11-94",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cf2ed3e46f7d98087f0a5a4d5573cd99621d1ab7d7e88df5134d20f8020d4d9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cf2ed3e46f7-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PHILLIPS: Yes, I believe the jury trial was constitutionally required, Justice Scalia."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2011-11-94",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cf2ed3e46f7d98087f0a5a4d5573cd99621d1ab7d7e88df5134d20f8020d4d9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cf2ed3e46f7-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: What do you make of this"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "States",
      "case_id": "2011-11-94",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cf2ed3e46f7d98087f0a5a4d5573cd99621d1ab7d7e88df5134d20f8020d4d9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cf2ed3e46f7-0001-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the author of the opinion in Ice was citing to an amicus brief filed by States, which supplied illustrations of fine statutes that it believed would be imperiled by a purely programmatic rule-based application of Apprendi."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2011-11-94",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cf2ed3e46f7d98087f0a5a4d5573cd99621d1ab7d7e88df5134d20f8020d4d9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cf2ed3e46f7-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: I thought it was a fairly simple algorithm. It says if the statutory penalty -- if a judge's factfinding can increase the statutory penalty, then that's a violation of the Sixth Amendment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2011-11-94",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cf2ed3e46f7d98087f0a5a4d5573cd99621d1ab7d7e88df5134d20f8020d4d9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cf2ed3e46f7-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: But, Mr. Dreeben, wasn't the core domain defined by whether it related to a specific statutory offense?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2011-11-94",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cf2ed3e46f7d98087f0a5a4d5573cd99621d1ab7d7e88df5134d20f8020d4d9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cf2ed3e46f7-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: A very -- very simple matter for the government to ask for jury findings on the questions at issue in this case, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2011-11-94",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cf2ed3e46f7d98087f0a5a4d5573cd99621d1ab7d7e88df5134d20f8020d4d9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cf2ed3e46f7-0001-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Mr. Dreeben, let's talk about Tyler."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2011-11-94",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cf2ed3e46f7d98087f0a5a4d5573cd99621d1ab7d7e88df5134d20f8020d4d9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cf2ed3e46f7-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Did it -- as a matter of statutory construction, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2011-11-94",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cf2ed3e46f7d98087f0a5a4d5573cd99621d1ab7d7e88df5134d20f8020d4d9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cf2ed3e46f7-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: You -- you don't think they believed in the Apprendi doctrine, either, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2011-11-94",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cf2ed3e46f7d98087f0a5a4d5573cd99621d1ab7d7e88df5134d20f8020d4d9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cf2ed3e46f7-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: What makes acceptable common law? Let's assume there weren't 50 States back then, but whatever the number was, 20 States, and 15 of them submitted it to the jury and 5 didn't. Does that mean there was no common law that this was generally submitted to the jury? If you can point to one case, that's enough to defeat the existence of a common law view?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chamber of Commerce",
      "case_id": "2011-11-94",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cf2ed3e46f7d98087f0a5a4d5573cd99621d1ab7d7e88df5134d20f8020d4d9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cf2ed3e46f7-0002-0005",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The amicus brief that was filed by the Chamber of Commerce demonstrates categorically they -- this -- this gets done every day, and it gets done effectively."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court Reporter",
      "case_id": "2011-11-94",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cf2ed3e46f7d98087f0a5a4d5573cd99621d1ab7d7e88df5134d20f8020d4d9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cf2ed3e46f7-0002-0006",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
    {
